\DOC LOCAL_MUL_P521_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MUL_P521_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
{LOCAL_MUL_P521_TAC} advances an {eventually}-style x86 machine-state goal past
an inlined (or called) P-521 field-multiplication block, replacing the current
symbolic state by a later one and recording the multiplication's result as a
new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} offset/adjustment used to locate the code block
to be stepped over (observed traces use small values such as 0, 1, 2); the
second is a {string list} of three names identifying the destination and the
two source operands of the multiplication, which fix the memory locations
appearing in the result assumption and the values it relates. The tactic
applies to goals of the shape below, where {P} is preserved and {s_j} is a
later state variable than {s_i}:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s_i
   =========================================  LOCAL_MUL_P521_TAC n ["dest"; "src1"; "src2"]
   A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. P s') s_j
}
\noindent In {A'}, the entire bundle describing the old state —
program-counter, code-loaded, register and memory-contents facts about {s_i},
together with the {MAYCHANGE ... s0 s_i} frame — is discharged and replaced by
the corresponding facts about {s_j}, with the frame extended by the component
written by the multiplication, plus a new memory-contents assumption giving the
destination's value in {s_j}. One further implication assumption is added, of
the form "both operands below the modulus implies the destination equals their
product reduced modulo the prime". Assumptions established by earlier steps are
retained, sometimes in a different order.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal, state-advancing behaviour,
always on goals of the above {eventually} shape with a large assumption list
describing an x86 state.

\ENDDOC
